      <h2>2. Step-by-Step Methodology: How Monthly Take-Home Pay is Computed</h2>
      <p>
        A frequent misconception among personnel is assuming that a 2.57x or 2.86x multiplier will double or triple their net in-hand monthly payslip. In reality, while the basic pay expands substantially, allowance percentages reset, and statutory deductions scale up simultaneously. The operational calculation follows a rigorous sequential formula:
      </p>

      <h3>Step 1: Baseline Recalibration</h3>
      <p>
        Your existing 7th CPC Basic Pay is multiplied by the designated fitment factor. For example, an employee drawing an entry basic of ₹35,400 in Level 6 under a 2.86x multiplier will see their basic adjust to ₹1,01,244. In practice, this figure is matched to the nearest index cell in the final notified Pay Matrix.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 2: Dearness Allowance (DA) Absorption & Reset</h3>
      <p>
        Upon official implementation, all accumulated Dearness Allowance is rolled directly into the revised base salary. Consequently, the Dearness Allowance rate <strong>resets to 0%</strong> on the date of effect. The DA counter then restarts its gradual biannual progression (notified every January and July) based on the 12-month moving average of the AICPI-IW.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 3: House Rent Allowance (HRA) Revision</h3>
      <p>
        Under Central Government guidelines, HRA is categorized by municipal population thresholds:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Class X Cities (Metros):</strong> 30% of revised basic pay (scaled upward from 24% after DA exceeded 50%).</li>
        <li><strong>Class Y Cities (Tier-2 Centers):</strong> 20% of revised basic pay.</li>
        <li><strong>Class Z Cities (Small Towns / Rural):</strong> 10% of revised basic pay.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Because HRA applies directly as a percentage of your newly expanded basic pay, the absolute rupee payout for house rent increases dramatically across all three classifications.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 4: Transport Allowance (TA) Calibration</h3>
      <p>
        Transport Allowance compensates for daily commuting costs. Base TA rates are classified by pay matrix tiers and city categories (higher transport allowance cities vs. other locations). While initial DA on TA resets to zero alongside basic DA, updated base TA allowances prevent any dip in transit compensation.
      </p>

      <h3>Step 5: Scaling Mandatory Statutory Deductions</h3>
      <p>
        A higher basic pay naturally expands statutory contributions:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>NPS / Unified Pension Scheme (UPS):</strong> Employees contribute a mandatory 10% of (Basic Pay + DA). An expanded base ensures a substantially larger monthly allocation toward your retirement corpus.</li>
        <li><strong>Healthcare (CGHS) & Group Insurance (CGEGIS):</strong> Central Government Health Scheme monthly contribution slabs and group life insurance subscriptions adjust upward according to your revised Pay Matrix level.</li>
        <li><strong>Income Tax (TDS):</strong> Higher gross earnings necessitate proactive tax planning across the New vs. Old Tax Regimes to maximize standard deductions and rebate advantages.</li>
      </ul>

